lariadou.1","L.Aiello.4","R.Stone.1","A.Renzi.1","T.Briant.2","Karen.Haughian.1","E.Flynn.3","S.P.Wakely.1","I.Khan.1","S.Frasca.1","G.Intini.6","Achim.Stahl.1","V.Tiwari.1","Alberto.Vecchio.1","G.L.Mansell.7","Joseph.C.Bayley.1","C.Graef.1","F.Sefkow.1","Brian.D.Fields.1","Hing.Tong.Cho.1","P.Puppo.1","Lijing.Shao.1","D.DeBra.1","P.Murray.1","H.Rafferty.1","Fabian.Meylahn.1","F.Donovan.2","D.Wood.4","A.De.Felice.1","Lukas.Terkowski.1","Maria.Cifaldi.1","Nima.Arkani.Hamed.1","M.A.Scheel.1","D.Keitel.1","Joseph.Betzwieser.1","M.Piendibene.1","S.Grunewald.20","H.U.Norgaard.Nielsen.1","T.L.Lam.2","M.Narain.1","D.Cohen.3","H.W.Wang.11","S.Penn.50","A.Marquina.8","Kamiel.Janssens.1","J.Kunz.1","D.Ugolini.42","B.Bolen.1","Jonathan.W.Richardson.1","Aneesh.V.Manohar.1","M.Yvert.2","O.A.Hannuksela.2","H.J.Blome.2","Bradley.E.Schaefer.1","S.Heinemeyer.1","R.J.E.Smith.7","T.R.Saravanan.1","G.Moreno.1","A.Dana.3","A.Cirone.8","Joris.Van.Heijningen.1","Micha.Moskovic.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"monochromefin.png","material":"preprint","caption":"\\textit{A two-dimensional projection of the parameter space for $H^2 = \\Lambda/3 =1$ for the 4D RNdS black hole. The boundary of the RNdS phase space or ``sharkfin\" corresponds to extremal conditions. At point $O$ lies pure dS space $(Q=M=0)$. Along the ON line, there is the Schwarzschild dS family of solutions $(Q=0, M>0),$ with the uncharged Nariai case at point $N$. The charged Nariai branch, at which $r_+ = r_c$, extends along NU; the extremal branch of ``cold\" solutions, with $r_- = r_+$, follows OU. On this branch, $Q \\sim Q_{ext} \\sim r_+$, with $Q_{ext}$ defined in Eq. (\\ref{eq:qext}). These branches terminate in the ``ultracold\" solution, where $r_- = r_+ = r_c$; Hawking temperature goes to zero and the local geometry is $\\mathbb{M}_2 \\times \\mathbb{S}^2$. }","label":"fig:sharkfin","source":"arxiv","key":"9276896810c90935f03d2ee8fd17dd92","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/9276896810c90935f03d2ee8fd17dd92"},{"filename":"plotE.png","material":"preprint","caption":"\\textit{ The QNM potential of a massive, charged scalar test field with $L^2_{dS} = 3 / \\Lambda =1$, $\\ell=1$, $q=0.1$, and $\\mu =0.1$. Here, $M=Q=1/4$, corresponding to point $E$ on Fig. \\ref{fig:sharkfin}: the charged Nariai solution where $M=Q=1/4$ and $r_+ = r_c$. The green line indicates the Cauchy horizon $r = r_c$; orange corresponds to the $r_+ = r_c$ line. For the ultracold case (point $U$), all horizons converge on this line at $r =3M/2 \\sim 0.408$ by Eq. (\\ref{eq:ultracold}). }","label":"fig:RNdSpotential-chNariai","source":"arxiv","key":"bb35ad5f330cc5e538c43ac86f8a03a6","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/bb35ad5f330cc5e538c43ac86f8a03a6"},{"filename":"colder.png","material":"preprint","caption":"\\textit{ The QNM potential of a massive, charged scalar test field with $L^2_{dS} = 3 / \\Lambda =1$, $\\ell=1$, $q=0.1$, and $\\mu =0.1$. Here, $M=0.2425$ and $Q=0.249$ such that $Q>M$, corresponding to the ``cold\" black hole case Green, blue, and orange delineators indicate Cauchy, event, and cosmological horizons, respectively. }","label":"fig:RNdSpotential-cold","source":"arxiv","key":"92ef8f9cc5bc04d38e1845e2c7c150c2","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/92ef8f9cc5bc04d38e1845e2c7c150c2"},{"filename":"ImwVSMu_MQc0.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : \\textit{ For $M=Q=0.104$ and $q=0$, the critical mass is $\\mu_{crit} \\sim \\pm 2.065$.}","label":"fig:critmass","source":"arxiv","key":"6a2c91ea84c1e969435dda80874aa62d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/6a2c91ea84c1e969435dda80874aa62d"},{"filename":"ImwVSMu_MQc01.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : \\textit{ For $M=Q=0.104$ and $q=0.1$ there is no longer an intersection point.}","label":"fig:critmass","source":"arxiv","key":"0f3fe7b2799d4c27d502e8aa2b4f2c3b","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/0f3fe7b2799d4c27d502e8aa2b4f2c3b"},{"filename":"plotbody1zerol.png","material":"preprint","caption":"\\textit{The QNM potential of a massive, charged scalar test field with $L^2_{dS} = 3 / \\Lambda =1$, $\\ell=0$, $q=0.1$, and $\\mu =0.1$. We set $M=0.112$ and $Q=0.016$ such that the potential corresponds to a position in the phase space central to $ON$. For fixed $Q$, increasing $M$ shrinks the amplitude of the potential. Note the valley following the peak of the barrier potential, which allows for superradiant amplification of the reflected wave. For $\\ell >0$, these disappear, as seen in Figs. \\ref{fig:RNdSpotential-chNariai} and \\ref{fig:RNdSpotential-cold}.  }","label":"fig:zeroL","source":"arxiv","key":"e10234dceafa3d53b4bda2da7ccb24b2","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e10234dceafa3d53b4bda2da7ccb24b2"},{"filename":"plotmu10c01l1unstable.png","material":"preprint","caption":"\\textit{Regions of instability for $\\omega_+$ (blue), $\\omega_c$ (orange), and both (magenta), where we have set $q=0.1$, $\\mu=10,$ and $\\ell =1$. We observe that unstable regions emerge when the orders of magnitude of $\\mu$ and $\\ell$ differ.}","label":"fig:mu10c01l1unstable","source":"arxiv","key":"20a6b18a9ce668a677fe13488ec2ad5a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/20a6b18a9ce668a677fe13488ec2ad5a"},{"filename":"plotmu10c01l10unstable.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : \\textit{Regions of instability.}","label":"fig:mu10c01l10SR","source":"arxiv","key":"e9b515befbf0c0313f9f4607ca330510","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e9b515befbf0c0313f9f4607ca330510"},{"filename":"plotSRmu10c01l10crop-ish2.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : \\textit{Regions reflecting superradiance}.","label":"fig:mu10c01l10SR","source":"arxiv","key":"e233574aa321610cbc7e976babffa870","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e233574aa321610cbc7e976babffa870"},{"filename":"SCCmu1c01L1.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Regions where $\\beta > \\frac{1}{2}$ for $\\mu=1$.","label":"fig:SCCmu10c0","source":"arxiv","key":"d0a55b3a6a2075c8cdfbf992e09c3daf","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/d0a55b3a6a2075c8cdfbf992e09c3daf"},{"filename":"SCCmu10c01L1.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : Regions where $\\beta > \\frac{1}{2}$ for $\\mu=10$.","label":"fig:SCCmu10c0","source":"arxiv","key":"b71ff3d228542a4a859afaf293ae8ca9","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b71ff3d228542a4a859afaf293ae8ca9"},{"filename":"mucritSCC.png","material":"preprint","caption":"\\textit{ A graphical indication of the critical mass $\\mu_{crit} \\sim 1.7$ for which $\\mathbb{I}m \\{ \\omega_+ \\}/\\vert \\kappa_- \\vert~>~1/2$. Here, $M=0.157$ and $Q=0.158$. Only in the extremal region $r_- \\sim r_+$ do we observe $\\mathbb{I}m \\{ \\omega_+ \\}/\\vert \\kappa_- \\vert $ values that approach $1/2$.}","label":"fig:mucritSCC","source":"arxiv","key":"2294164b1d31b3f56744955beebbe149","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/2294164b1d31b3f56744955beebbe149"}],"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology","source":"arxiv"},{"term":"Theory-HEP","source":"arxiv"}],"first_author":{"emails":["chrysostomou@ip2i.in2p3.fr"],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/02avf8f85"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/04z6c2n17"}],"full_name":"Chrysostomou, Anna","last_name":"Chrysostomou","first_name":"Anna","recid":1875636},"control_number":2708740,"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Chrysostomou:2023jiv"],"abstracts":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"Rich physics can be divined from charged black holes subjected to extremal conditions. When applied in conjunction with principles like Weak Cosmic Censorship, this naturally leads to constraints on the mass and charge of the black hole. However, more nuanced principles such as the Weak Gravity Conjecture (WGC) and the recently proposed Festina-Lente (FL) bound can provide, respectively, upper and lower bounds on elementary charged particles. In this study, we examine the quasinormal modes (QNMs) exhibited by a massive scalar test field carrying an electric charge, oscillating in the outer region of the black hole. These modes are subjected to the constraints imposed by the FL and WGC bounds. Our analysis provides insight into the behaviour of QNMs, particularly in regions that approach the extremal conditions of the black hole. Notably, in these regimes, the stability of the modes becomes precarious, particularly in the presence of a positive cosmological constant. The implications of our findings are far-reaching and significant. They extend from safeguarding the principles of cosmic censorship to addressing the structural stability of the black hole's interior. Our semi-classical analysis presents compelling evidence suggesting that Strong Cosmic Censorship may be violated for black holes that are in close proximity to extremality within the context of Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter (RNdS) geometries.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"source":"arXiv","title":"Reissner-Nordström black holes in de Sitter space-time: bounds with quasinormal frequencies"}],"curated":true},"id":"2708740","created":"2023-10-12T02:44:46.178067+00:00"},{"updated":"2026-08-18T11:55:14.012821+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3183727?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3183727?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3183727?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3183727?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3183727?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3183727?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A3183727"},"metadata":{"authors":[{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"College of Physics and Materials Science, Tianjin Normal University, Tianjin 300387, China"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"liu, hang","full_name":"Liu, Hang","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1482842"},"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"Hang.Liu.1"}],"last_name":"Liu","first_name":"Hang","u