.Mei.Kuang.1","F.Saueressig.1","S.Fernando.1","J.Mazza.1","J.Lopes.Costa.1","Houwen.Wu.1","P.Singh.4","Guangzhou.Guo.1","Yu.Sen.Zhou.1","C.M.Tang.2","S.C.Park.1","Liang.Bi.Wu.1","Marija.Tomasevic.1","M.Rahman.6","M.Van.de.Moortel.1","R.Penrose.1","Hang.Liu.1","Yu.Peng.Zhang.1","Hing.Tong.Cho.1","Valeri.P.Frolov.1","A.Ori.2","M.Opala.1","Bin.Wang.2","A.Courty.1","Ioannis.Soranidis.1","Z.Li.341","O.J.C.Dias.1","Zhao.Yi.Xu.1","Li.Ming.Cao.1","A.Jansen.2","R.Dal.Bosco.Fontana.1","Helvi.Witek.1","Hong.Guo.1","Edgardo.Franzin.1","P.Li.4","J.Luk.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"parameterspace.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The parameter space of ABGB-dS black hole. The interior of the parameter space (shaded area) represents black hole parameters which guarantee three distinct horizons, while at most two horizons are allowed on the boundary. We have $r_-=r_+$ on the upper  black boundary  curve, and $r_+=r_c$ on the lower black boundary curve.","label":"fig0","source":"arxiv","key":"bb8df828121f88247b76f4b775b074f8","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/bb8df828121f88247b76f4b775b074f8"},{"filename":"purelyimaginaryvscomplex.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The behaviors of imaginary part of fundamental  QNF of two classes of QNMs with $l=0$ under the change of scalar mass $\\mu$. The dashed orange curve denotes the purely imaginary QNF, and solid orange curve denotes the complex QNF, i.e. the real part is non-vanishing.","label":"fig1","source":"arxiv","key":"331672f6f48a47bcd9466ab45eaa8536","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/331672f6f48a47bcd9466ab45eaa8536"},{"filename":"0.09Lmax.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The behaviors of $\\beta_{\\mathrm{PS,dS, NE}}$ related to  fundamental PS modes (blue solid, $l=10$), dS modes (red dashed, $l=1$) and NE modes (green dot-dashed, $l=0$) of massless scalar field perturbation as a function of $Q/Q_{\\mathrm{max}}$. In our ABGB-dS black hole case, $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.09,0.27,0.63$ correspond to $\\Lambda=0.02,0.06,0.14$ in RN-dS spacetime \\cite{Cardoso:2017soq}, respectively. The last plot with $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.8$ can be compared with the result in \\cite{Liu:2019lon}.","label":"fig2","source":"arxiv","key":"836e79636179eadb18f7959b73c3bf50","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/836e79636179eadb18f7959b73c3bf50"},{"filename":"0.27Lmax.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The behaviors of $\\beta_{\\mathrm{PS,dS, NE}}$ related to  fundamental PS modes (blue solid, $l=10$), dS modes (red dashed, $l=1$) and NE modes (green dot-dashed, $l=0$) of massless scalar field perturbation as a function of $Q/Q_{\\mathrm{max}}$. In our ABGB-dS black hole case, $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.09,0.27,0.63$ correspond to $\\Lambda=0.02,0.06,0.14$ in RN-dS spacetime \\cite{Cardoso:2017soq}, respectively. The last plot with $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.8$ can be compared with the result in \\cite{Liu:2019lon}.","label":"fig2","source":"arxiv","key":"14e0ff336e391cf73d24805a20aafe4a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/14e0ff336e391cf73d24805a20aafe4a"},{"filename":"0.63Lmax.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The behaviors of $\\beta_{\\mathrm{PS,dS, NE}}$ related to  fundamental PS modes (blue solid, $l=10$), dS modes (red dashed, $l=1$) and NE modes (green dot-dashed, $l=0$) of massless scalar field perturbation as a function of $Q/Q_{\\mathrm{max}}$. In our ABGB-dS black hole case, $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.09,0.27,0.63$ correspond to $\\Lambda=0.02,0.06,0.14$ in RN-dS spacetime \\cite{Cardoso:2017soq}, respectively. The last plot with $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.8$ can be compared with the result in \\cite{Liu:2019lon}.","label":"fig2","source":"arxiv","key":"4611185f086854c6b3f262312f61114c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/4611185f086854c6b3f262312f61114c"},{"filename":"0.8Lmax.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The behaviors of $\\beta_{\\mathrm{PS,dS, NE}}$ related to  fundamental PS modes (blue solid, $l=10$), dS modes (red dashed, $l=1$) and NE modes (green dot-dashed, $l=0$) of massless scalar field perturbation as a function of $Q/Q_{\\mathrm{max}}$. In our ABGB-dS black hole case, $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.09,0.27,0.63$ correspond to $\\Lambda=0.02,0.06,0.14$ in RN-dS spacetime \\cite{Cardoso:2017soq}, respectively. The last plot with $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.8$ can be compared with the result in \\cite{Liu:2019lon}.","label":"fig2","source":"arxiv","key":"c712d73408235dfbc54da5edaebc40db","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/c712d73408235dfbc54da5edaebc40db"},{"filename":"mu0.2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The behaviors of $-\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\omega/\\kappa_{-}$ from  fundamental  massive QNMs with angular number $l=0,1,3,5$ and $10$  as a function of $Q/Q_{\\mathrm{max}}$ for several scalar mass values at  $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.27$.","label":"fig3","source":"arxiv","key":"57a63ae3c69b420a221db34516bf7374","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/57a63ae3c69b420a221db34516bf7374"},{"filename":"mu0.4.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The behaviors of $-\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\omega/\\kappa_{-}$ from  fundamental  massive QNMs with angular number $l=0,1,3,5$ and $10$  as a function of $Q/Q_{\\mathrm{max}}$ for several scalar mass values at  $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.27$.","label":"fig3","source":"arxiv","key":"8e303e13c34894de86da87a174d4a931","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/8e303e13c34894de86da87a174d4a931"},{"filename":"mu0.6.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The behaviors of $-\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\omega/\\kappa_{-}$ from  fundamental  massive QNMs with angular number $l=0,1,3,5$ and $10$  as a function of $Q/Q_{\\mathrm{max}}$ for several scalar mass values at  $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.27$.","label":"fig3","source":"arxiv","key":"20a8eb2fc06adbf16812bfcb2fb77c55","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/20a8eb2fc06adbf16812bfcb2fb77c55"},{"filename":"mu0.8.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The behaviors of $-\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\omega/\\kappa_{-}$ from  fundamental  massive QNMs with angular number $l=0,1,3,5$ and $10$  as a function of $Q/Q_{\\mathrm{max}}$ for several scalar mass values at  $\\Lambda/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.27$.","label":"fig3","source":"arxiv","key":"017d7df366c74666e069abbd9162b437","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/017d7df366c74666e069abbd9162b437"}],"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology","source":"arxiv"}],"preprint_date":"2026-07-27","author_count":2,"first_author":{"emails":["hangliu@sjtu.edu.cn"],"full_name":"Liu, Hang","last_name":"Liu","first_name":"Hang","recid":1482842},"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"27 pages, 4 figures"}],"control_number":3183727,"earliest_date":"2026-07-27","document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Liu:2026vjv"],"abstracts":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"We investigate the quasinormal modes (QNMs) of scalar perturbations and strong cosmic censorship (SCC) in regular Ayón-Beato-García-Bronnikov-de Sitter (ABGB-dS) black hole spacetime. The main motivation of this work is to clarify whether the regularization of black hole core, which removes the central singularity, can also change the fate of SCC connected to the Cauchy horizon. We first study the dependence of the scalar QNM frequency (QNF) on the scalar mass and black hole parameters. For angular number $l=0$, a small scalar mass can give rise to a slowly decaying mode which is purely imaginary. We also find that the black hole charge and cosmological constant affect the QNM spectrum in qualitatively opposite ways. We then examine SCC under massless and massive scalar perturbations. For massless perturbations, the QNMs spectrum can be organized into photon sphere (PS), de Sitter (dS) and near-extremal (NE) families. Compared with the SCC in the Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter (RN-dS) case, SCC is generally more easily violated in the ABGB-dS spacetime, although this tendency is reversed for sufficiently large cosmological constant. For massive scalar perturbations, we find that the scalar mass does not restore SCC. Instead, it can enhance the violation by driving the system into the regime $β>1$. At last, we discuss what we may expect from the violation of SCC in a regular black hole spacetime.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"source":"arXiv","title":"Scalar perturbations and strong cosmic censorship in a regular ABGB-de Sitter black hole spacetime"}],"facet_author_name":["1750574_Hong Guo","1482842_Hang Liu"],"core":false,"license":[{"license":"arXiv nonexclusive-distrib 1.0","material":"preprint","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:3183727","updated":"2026-08-18T11:55:14.012821"},"curated":false,"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["gr-qc"],"value":"2607.24119"}]},"id":"3183727","created":"2026-07-28T04:30:01.106808+00:00"},{"updated":"2025-01-17T08:10:17.169260+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2774616?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2774616?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2774616?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2774616?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2774616?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2774616?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A2774616"},"metadata":{"publication_info":[{"journal_volume":"07","artid":"113","year":2024,"journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1213103"},"page_start":"113","journal_title":"JHEP"}],"authors":[{"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"GRID","value":"grid.5491.9"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/01ryk1543"}],"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"STAG research centre and Mathematical Sciences, University of Southampton, University Road, SO17 1BJ Southampton, UK"}],"full_na