mpedakis and H.O. Silva, Eikonal quasinormal modes of black holes beyond General Relativity, Phys. Rev. D 100 (2019) 044040 [, https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1906.05455] []."}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1739814"}}],"number_of_pages":54,"preprint_date":"2024-04-04","author_count":3,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"55 pages, 13 figures"}],"earliest_date":"2024-04-04","refereed":true,"facet_author_name":["1892901_Alex Davey","1021407_Oscar J.C. Dias","2774618_David Sola Gil"],"core":true,"license":[{"license":"CC-BY-4.0","imposing":"Springer","url":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"},{"license":"CC BY 4.0","material":"preprint","url":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:2774616","updated":"2025-01-17T08:10:17.169260"},"journal_title_variants":["JHEP"],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"value":"2404.03724"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["I.G.Moss.1","Silke.Klemm.1","R.Monteiro.1","Hector.O.Silva.1","C.F.B.Macedo.1","S.Dyatlov.1","W.Israel.1","Chiang.Mei.Chen.1","S.J.Oh.1","M.Zilhao.1","S.A.Teukolsky.1","Antonia.M.Frassino.1","K.D.Kokkotas.1","R.C.Myers.1","W.H.Press.1","F.Mellor.1","S.L.Detweiler.1","D.A.Nichols.1","Hisao.Suzuki.1","Hong.Bao.Zhang.1","N.Oshita.1","Roman.A.Konoplya.1","R.Emparan.1","S.Hod.1","Arman.Tursunov.1","Yanbei.Chen.1","Kyriakos.Destounis.1","M.Kolanowski.2","Paul.K.Townsend.1","P.R.Brady.1","P.Pani.1","Z.Zhang.219","Christiane.Klein.1","A.Davey.1","A.Strominger.1","R.H.Price.1","G.Carullo.11","T.Nishioka.1","A.Lopez.Ortega.1","G.W.Gibbons.1","K.M.Simpson.1","H.S.Reall.1","Zhen.Zhong.2","G.T.Horowitz.1","Bin.Wang.1","E.Takasugi.1","V.Cardoso.1","M.Zajacek.1","Sarp.Akcay.1","Huan.Yang.1","P.Breitenlohner.1","Yuyu.Mo.1","W.Del.Pozzo.2","E.Papantonopoulos.1","M.Dafermos.1","E.Poisson.1","P.Hintz.1","J.E.Santos.1","R.A.Matzner.1","Demetrios.Christodoulou.1","M.Giesler.1","M.Casals.2","F.Eperon.1","R.M.Wald.1","U.Yurtsever.1","Takashi.Nakamura.2","Yu.Tian.1","B.Way.1","J.Natario.1","S.R.Dolan.1","A.Franzen.1","S.Hollands.1","E.W.Leaver.1","Zi.Yu.Tang.1","Hiroshi.Umetsu.1","Clifford.Martin.Will.1","J.Long.1","Thomas.E.Hartman.1","Anil.Zenginoglu.1","G.Cognola.1","M.M.Caldarelli.1","Y.Shlapentokh.Rothman.1","A.Vasy.1","M.Durkee.1","N.Andersson.1","R.Bousso.1","Stephen.W.Hawking.1","M.Godazgar.1","Vijay.Balasubramanian.1","J.Lopes.Costa.1","M.Soltani.1","K.Glampedakis.1","A.O.Starinets.1","B.Mashhoon.1","Marija.Tomasevic.1","A.B.Zimmerman.1","R.Penrose.1","Hang.Liu.1","A.Ishibashi.1","J.Zahn.1","Raimon.Luna.1","A.Ori.2","G.Compere.1","Bin.Chen.2","R.Gregory.1","Grant.N.Remmen.1","K.Murata.1","G.Sarosi.1","Maren.Stein.1","P.Rodgers.1","O.J.C.Dias.1","Fan.Zhang.3","V.Ferrari.1","A.Jansen.2","B.Carter.1","Bo.Ning.1","M.S.Churilova.1","C.I.S.Marinho.1","P.Figueras.1","L.Mezincescu.1","D.Z.Freedman.1","A.Kar.1","J.Luk.1","M.Sasaki.1","S.Patrick.2","Danny.Laghi.1","Emanuele.Berti.1","A.Zhidenko.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"fig1_knds_penrose.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Section of the Penrose diagram (common to RNdS, Kerr-dS and KNdS spacetimes) that is relevant for the SCC discussion. The future event and future cosmological horizons are labelled by $\\mathcal{H}^+$ and $\\mathcal{H}^+_c$ and the blue lines represent the right and left future Cauchy horizons $\\mathcal{C}\\mathcal{H}_{L,R}$ respectively. The QNMs in this spacetime blow up along the red edge and along the past cosmological horizon (lower right black edge of region I)~\\cite{Dias:2018ynt}. The green line represents an example of a Cauchy surface $\\Sigma$.","label":"fig:kerrdSspacetime","source":"arxiv","key":"abf23ef8df514cd22f00262b8dc3d8c8","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/abf23ef8df514cd22f00262b8dc3d8c8"},{"filename":"fig2_knds_parameter_space_1.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Region of the dimensionless parameter space $(M/L, a/L, Q/L)$ where regular KNdS black holes exist (from two distinct viewpoints in the left/right plots to clearly capture all boundary surfaces). The cyan $ABC$ surface with $a=0$ (or $\\Theta=0$) describes regular RNdS black holes~\\cite{Bousso:1996pn}, the orange $ABD$ surface  with $Q=0$ (or $\\Theta=\\pi/2$) describes Kerr-dS~\\cite{Akcay:2010vt, Gregory:2021ozs}, the green $ACD$ surface describes extremal KNdS (with $r_-=r_+$ \\ie $\\calr = 1$) and the red $BCD$ surface describes KNdS black holes in the Nariai limit (with $r_+=r_c$ \\ie  $y_+ = 1$).  The $AB$ purple line describes Schwarzschild-dS black holes, the $CD$ thick black line describes  extremal KNdS in the Nariai limit (\\ie with $r_-=r_+=r_c$ or, equivalently, with $(\\calr ,y_+ )= (1,1)$). Point $A=(0,0,0)$ describes the pure de Sitter solution, point $B=(\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}},0,0)$ describes the Nariai Schwarzschild-dS black hole, point  $C=(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{3}}, 0, \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}})$ describes  extremal and Nariai RNdS (\\ie with $a=0$ and $r_-=r_+=r_c$), and point  $D=(\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{26 \\sqrt{3}-45}, 2- \\sqrt{3},0)$ describes extremal Nariai Kerr-dS black holes  (\\ie with $Q=0$ and $r_-=r_+=r_c$).","label":"fig:KNdS_parameterspace","source":"arxiv","key":"e8d19a4b72e1715c383f4582e8e11695","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e8d19a4b72e1715c383f4582e8e11695"},{"filename":"fig2_knds_parameter_space_2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Region of the dimensionless parameter space $(M/L, a/L, Q/L)$ where regular KNdS black holes exist (from two distinct viewpoints in the left/right plots to clearly capture all boundary surfaces). The cyan $ABC$ surface with $a=0$ (or $\\Theta=0$) describes regular RNdS black holes~\\cite{Bousso:1996pn}, the orange $ABD$ surface  with $Q=0$ (or $\\Theta=\\pi/2$) describes Kerr-dS~\\cite{Akcay:2010vt, Gregory:2021ozs}, the green $ACD$ surface describes extremal KNdS (with $r_-=r_+$ \\ie $\\calr = 1$) and the red $BCD$ surface describes KNdS black holes in the Nariai limit (with $r_+=r_c$ \\ie  $y_+ = 1$).  The $AB$ purple line describes Schwarzschild-dS black holes, the $CD$ thick black line describes  extremal KNdS in the Nariai limit (\\ie with $r_-=r_+=r_c$ or, equivalently, with $(\\calr ,y_+ )= (1,1)$). Point $A=(0,0,0)$ describes the pure de Sitter solution, point $B=(\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}},0,0)$ describes the Nariai Schwarzschild-dS black hole, point  $C=(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{3}}, 0, \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}})$ describes  extremal and Nariai RNdS (\\ie with $a=0$ and $r_-=r_+=r_c$), and point  $D=(\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{26 \\sqrt{3}-45}, 2- \\sqrt{3},0)$ describes extremal Nariai Kerr-dS black holes  (\\ie with $Q=0$ and $r_-=r_+=r_c$).","label":"fig:KNdS_parameterspace","source":"arxiv","key":"e4cffa4db7dadbf3f34eb19ab502d815","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e4cffa4db7dadbf3f34eb19ab502d815"},{"filename":"fig3_ps_nh_modes_ml1_r099_yp05_im.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency (in units of the cosmological horizon length $r_c$) of the $n=0$ NH (blue diamonds), $n=1$ NH (light blue diamonds) and $n=0$ PS (orange disks) modes for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 1$ on KNdS with $\\calr = 0.99, y_+ = 1/2$ as a function of $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$. The $n = 0$ ($n=1$) MAE approximation $\\omega_{\\MAE}$ of the NH modes is described by a dot-dashed purple (light purple) curve. The $n = 0$ ($n=1$) eikonal approximation $\\omega_{\\hbox{\\tiny eik}}$ of the PS modes are given by a green (light green) dashed curve. The red curve is the critical curve ${\\rm Im}(\\omega r_c)=-\\beta_c \\kappa_-$ with $\\beta_c=1/2$ relevant for the SCC discussion, and the $n=0$ PS curve is above it for for $\\Theta\\gtrsim 0.87$. The thin vertical dark yellow line at $\\Theta=\\Theta_c \\sim 0.73$ describes the $\\Theta$ where $\\delta^2 = 0$, with $\\delta^2<0$ for smaller $\\Theta$ values and $\\delta^2>0$ for larger $\\Theta$. The right panel is a zoom of the left panel, in a range of ${\\rm Im}(\\omega r_c)$ where we better see the orange PS mode (and green eikonal curve) crossing the red curve.","label":"fig:PS_NH_modes_ml1_R099_yp05_im","source":"arxiv","key":"8f07a134c5ac8f0bfd65d263dcd28213","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/8f07a134c5ac8f0bfd65d263dcd28213"},{"filename":"fig3_zoomed_ps_nh_modes_ml1_r099_yp05_im.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency (in units of the cosmological horizon length $r_c$) of the $n=0$ NH (blue diamonds), $n=1$ NH (light blue diamonds) and $n=0$ PS (orange disks) modes for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 1$ on KNdS with $\\calr = 0.99, y_+ = 1/2$ as a function of $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$. The $n = 0$ ($n=1$) MAE approximation $\\omega_{\\MAE}$ of the NH modes is described by a dot-dashed purple (light purple) curve. The $n = 0$ ($n=1$) eikonal approximation $\\omega_{\\hbox{\\tiny eik}}$ of the PS modes are given by a green (light green) dashed curve. The red curve is the critical curve ${\\rm Im}(\\omega r_c)=-\\beta_c \\kappa_-$ with $\\beta_c=1/2$ relevant for the SCC discussion, and the $n=0$ PS curve is above it for for $\\Theta\\gtrsim 0.87$. The thin vertical dark yellow line at $\\Theta=\\Theta_c \\sim 0.73$ describes the $\\Theta$ where $\\delta^2 = 0$, with $\\delta^2<0$ for smaller $\\Theta$ values and $\\delta^2>0$ for larger $\\Theta$. The right panel is a zoom of the left panel, in a range of ${\\rm Im}(\\omega r_c)$ where we better see the orange PS mode (and green eikonal curve) crossing the red curve.","label":"fig:PS_NH_modes_ml1_R099_yp05_im","source":"arxiv","key":"f10823352d23c5887c5ec19243da5109","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f10823352d23c5887c5ec19243da5109"},{"filename":"fig4_ps_nh_repulsion_m1l1_r994sq_yp05_im.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 1$ on KNdS with $y_+ = 1/2$ as a function of $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ for three different values of $\\calr$ (top to bottom). \\textbf{Left column:} constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). \\textbf{Right column:} zoomed in plots near the `intersection' or eigenvalue repulsion regions for constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). The colour code is the same as in Fig.~\\ref{fig:PS_NH_modes_ml1_R099_yp05_im} but this time we just display the $n=0$ overtone modes.","label":"fig:eigenvalue_repulsion","source":"arxiv","key":"ecfcb4b9cf3703be997f2329f4752e1f","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/ecfcb4b9cf3703be997f2329f4752e1f"},{"filename":"fig4_ps_nh_zoomedrepulsion_m1l1_r994sq_yp05_im.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 1$ on KNdS with $y_+ = 1/2$ as a function of $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ for three different values of $\\calr$ (top to bottom). \\textbf{Left column:} constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). \\textbf{Right column:} zoomed in plots near the `intersection' or eigenvalue repulsion regions for constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). The colour code is the same as in Fig.~\\ref{fig:PS_NH_modes_ml1_R099_yp05_im} but this time we just display the $n=0$ overtone modes.","label":"fig:eigenvalue_repulsion","source":"arxiv","key":"d5382ac3d3a910db613b49d3b4b2e269","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/d5382ac3d3a910db613b49d3b4b2e269"},{"filename":"fig4_ps_nh_repulsion_m1l1_r995sq_yp05_im.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 1$ on KNdS with $y_+ = 1/2$ as a function of $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ for three different values of $\\calr$ (top to bottom). \\textbf{Left column:} constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). \\textbf{Right column:} zoomed in plots near the `intersection' or eigenvalue repulsion regions for constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). The colour code is the same as in Fig.~\\ref{fig:PS_NH_modes_ml1_R099_yp05_im} but this time we just display the $n=0$ overtone modes.","label":"fig:eigenvalue_repulsion","source":"arxiv","key":"517d23663b4e984bd41bfb632cdcb491","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/517d23663b4e984bd41bfb632cdcb491"},{"filename":"fig4_ps_nh_zoomedrepulsion_m1l1_r995sq_yp05_im.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 1$ on KNdS with $y_+ = 1/2$ as a function of $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ for three different values of $\\calr$ (top to bottom). \\textbf{Left column:} constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). \\textbf{Right column:} zoomed in plots near the `intersection' or eigenvalue repulsion regions for constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). The colour code is the same as in Fig.~\\ref{fig:PS_NH_modes_ml1_R099_yp05_im} but this time we just display the $n=0$ overtone modes.","label":"fig:eigenvalue_repulsion","source":"arxiv","key":"c09c7677cf565ca6818a9a3d12fade9b","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/c09c7677cf565ca6818a9a3d12fade9b"},{"filename":"fig4_ps_nh_repulsion_m1l1_r996sq_yp05_im.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 1$ on KNdS with $y_+ = 1/2$ as a function of $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ for three different values of $\\calr$ (top to bottom). \\textbf{Left column:} constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). \\textbf{Right column:} zoomed in plots near the `intersection' or eigenvalue repulsion regions for constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). The colour code is the same as in Fig.~\\ref{fig:PS_NH_modes_ml1_R099_yp05_im} but this time we just display the $n=0$ overtone modes.","label":"fig:eigenvalue_repulsion","source":"arxiv","key":"8cd46cb361ba8c1406ae67b7e6055db2","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/8cd46cb361ba8c1406ae67b7e6055db2"},{"filename":"fig4_ps_nh_zoomedrepulsion_m1l1_r996sq_yp05_im.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 1$ on KNdS with $y_+ = 1/2$ as a function of $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ for three different values of $\\calr$ (top to bottom). \\textbf{Left column:} constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). \\textbf{Right column:} zoomed in plots near the `intersection' or eigenvalue repulsion regions for constant $\\calr = 0.994$ (top), $\\calr = 0.995$ (middle) and $\\calr = 0.996$ (bottom). The colour code is the same as in Fig.~\\ref{fig:PS_NH_modes_ml1_R099_yp05_im} but this time we just display the $n=0$ overtone modes.","label":"fig:eigenvalue_repulsion","source":"arxiv","key":"e49c01eb6861b55aa44050b323829afb","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e49c01eb6861b55aa44050b323829afb"},{"filename":"fig5_rndsm0l0sliceatr095allpoints.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary (left panels) and real (right panels) parts of the frequency as a function of the off-Nariai parameter $y_+$ for ($q =\\mu = 0$) $m=\\ell=0$ QNMs in RNdS ($\\Theta=0$) with fixed off-extremality parameter $\\calr = 0.95$. The orange disks represent $n=0$ PS modes with complex frequencies and we also display the first two purely imaginary dominant modes with blue diamonds (since they have ${\\rm Re}(\\omega r_c)=0$ we simply do not display them in the right panels for the sake of clarity). The purely imaginary modes are initially closely followed by the MAE frequencies with $n=0$ (purple dot-dashed curve) and $n=1$ (lighter dot-dashed lines). The second row gives zoom plots of the top panel in the regions where interesting splittings and/or mergers happen. Here, the inset plots are further zooms around the region $y_+ \\in [0.87, 0.9]$.","label":"fig:m0l0RNdSR095slice","source":"arxiv","key":"955167e6cb779ad0d931f63f41fcc7e8","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/955167e6cb779ad0d931f63f41fcc7e8"},{"filename":"fig5_realrndsm0l0sliceatr095allpoints.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary (left panels) and real (right panels) parts of the frequency as a function of the off-Nariai parameter $y_+$ for ($q =\\mu = 0$) $m=\\ell=0$ QNMs in RNdS ($\\Theta=0$) with fixed off-extremality parameter $\\calr = 0.95$. The orange disks represent $n=0$ PS modes with complex frequencies and we also display the first two purely imaginary dominant modes with blue diamonds (since they have ${\\rm Re}(\\omega r_c)=0$ we simply do not display them in the right panels for the sake of clarity). The purely imaginary modes are initially closely followed by the MAE frequencies with $n=0$ (purple dot-dashed curve) and $n=1$ (lighter dot-dashed lines). The second row gives zoom plots of the top panel in the regions where interesting splittings and/or mergers happen. Here, the inset plots are further zooms around the region $y_+ \\in [0.87, 0.9]$.","label":"fig:m0l0RNdSR095slice","source":"arxiv","key":"db8fe6458a83a219a9250b0dd838c918","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/db8fe6458a83a219a9250b0dd838c918"},{"filename":"fig5_endtailwithinsetplot.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary (left panels) and real (right panels) parts of the frequency as a function of the off-Nariai parameter $y_+$ for ($q =\\mu = 0$) $m=\\ell=0$ QNMs in RNdS ($\\Theta=0$) with fixed off-extremality parameter $\\calr = 0.95$. The orange disks represent $n=0$ PS modes with complex frequencies and we also display the first two purely imaginary dominant modes with blue diamonds (since they have ${\\rm Re}(\\omega r_c)=0$ we simply do not display them in the right panels for the sake of clarity). The purely imaginary modes are initially closely followed by the MAE frequencies with $n=0$ (purple dot-dashed curve) and $n=1$ (lighter dot-dashed lines). The second row gives zoom plots of the top panel in the regions where interesting splittings and/or mergers happen. Here, the inset plots are further zooms around the region $y_+ \\in [0.87, 0.9]$.","label":"fig:m0l0RNdSR095slice","source":"arxiv","key":"00f41bb787b9200f3f24923c8553cfce","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/00f41bb787b9200f3f24923c8553cfce"},{"filename":"fig5_reendtailwithinsetplot.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary (left panels) and real (right panels) parts of the frequency as a function of the off-Nariai parameter $y_+$ for ($q =\\mu = 0$) $m=\\ell=0$ QNMs in RNdS ($\\Theta=0$) with fixed off-extremality parameter $\\calr = 0.95$. The orange disks represent $n=0$ PS modes with complex frequencies and we also display the first two purely imaginary dominant modes with blue diamonds (since they have ${\\rm Re}(\\omega r_c)=0$ we simply do not display them in the right panels for the sake of clarity). The purely imaginary modes are initially closely followed by the MAE frequencies with $n=0$ (purple dot-dashed curve) and $n=1$ (lighter dot-dashed lines). The second row gives zoom plots of the top panel in the regions where interesting splittings and/or mergers happen. Here, the inset plots are further zooms around the region $y_+ \\in [0.87, 0.9]$.","label":"fig:m0l0RNdSR095slice","source":"arxiv","key":"114ad0a69087c34f4d858cf98cf78d23","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/114ad0a69087c34f4d858cf98cf78d23"},{"filename":"fig6_ps_nh_modes_ml0_r095_yp05_im.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency as a function of $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 0$ on KNdS with $\\calr = 0.95$, with two different values of $y_+$: $y_+=0.5$ (left plot) and $y_+=0.9$ (right plot). \\textbf{Left plot}: for $y_+ = 1/2$, we show the $n=0$ NH (blue diamonds), $n=1$ NH (lighter blue diamonds) and $n=0$ PS (orange disks) modes. The $n = 0$ ($n=1$) MAE approximation $\\omega_{\\MAE}$ to the NH modes is given by a dot-dashed purple (lighter purple) curve. The red curve is the critical curve ${\\rm Im}(\\omega r_c)=-\\beta_c \\kappa_-$ with $\\beta_c=1/2$ relevant for the SCC discussion. \\textbf{Right plot}: for $y_+ = 0.9$ and with the same colour coding, we show that the dominant (blue diamond) NH mode enforces SCC for all $\\Theta$ since its curve is above the critical red curve ${\\rm Im}(\\omega r_c)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\kappa_-\\,$.","label":"fig:PS_NH_ml0_R095_yp05_im","source":"arxiv","key":"5b0b5f2613fe17b77ef24958dfcbebcf","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5b0b5f2613fe17b77ef24958dfcbebcf"},{"filename":"fig6_ps_nh_modes_ml0_r095_yp09_im.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency as a function of $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 0$ on KNdS with $\\calr = 0.95$, with two different values of $y_+$: $y_+=0.5$ (left plot) and $y_+=0.9$ (right plot). \\textbf{Left plot}: for $y_+ = 1/2$, we show the $n=0$ NH (blue diamonds), $n=1$ NH (lighter blue diamonds) and $n=0$ PS (orange disks) modes. The $n = 0$ ($n=1$) MAE approximation $\\omega_{\\MAE}$ to the NH modes is given by a dot-dashed purple (lighter purple) curve. The red curve is the critical curve ${\\rm Im}(\\omega r_c)=-\\beta_c \\kappa_-$ with $\\beta_c=1/2$ relevant for the SCC discussion. \\textbf{Right plot}: for $y_+ = 0.9$ and with the same colour coding, we show that the dominant (blue diamond) NH mode enforces SCC for all $\\Theta$ since its curve is above the critical red curve ${\\rm Im}(\\omega r_c)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\kappa_-\\,$.","label":"fig:PS_NH_ml0_R095_yp05_im","source":"arxiv","key":"5f9b74f3bed4303e89eaef59ace11963","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5f9b74f3bed4303e89eaef59ace11963"},{"filename":"fig7_eikonal_verification_yp01_extended.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Contour plots of $\\beta_{\\rm PS}$ for exact numerical $m = \\ell = 10$ PS modes as a function of   $\\{\\calr, \\Theta=\\arctan(a/Q)\\}$ for KNdS families with fixed $y_+ = 0.1$ (top-left panel), $y_+ = 0.5$ (top-right panel) and $y_+ = 0.9$ (bottom panel). In each plot, the critical curve with  $\\beta_{\\rm PS} = \\frac{1}{2}$ is  the red curve (around the top left corner), and the dashed green line identifies the eikonal prediction $\\beta_{\\rm eik}=\\frac{1}{2}$.","label":"fig:eikonal_verification","source":"arxiv","key":"eb3f213dd8e69ea9b33d297e7533bccd","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/eb3f213dd8e69ea9b33d297e7533bccd"},{"filename":"fig7_eikonal_verification_yp05_extended.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Contour plots of $\\beta_{\\rm PS}$ for exact numerical $m = \\ell = 10$ PS modes as a function of   $\\{\\calr, \\Theta=\\arctan(a/Q)\\}$ for KNdS families with fixed $y_+ = 0.1$ (top-left panel), $y_+ = 0.5$ (top-right panel) and $y_+ = 0.9$ (bottom panel). In each plot, the critical curve with  $\\beta_{\\rm PS} = \\frac{1}{2}$ is  the red curve (around the top left corner), and the dashed green line identifies the eikonal prediction $\\beta_{\\rm eik}=\\frac{1}{2}$.","label":"fig:eikonal_verification","source":"arxiv","key":"0bde2c48831a1f25605529fe92c95961","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/0bde2c48831a1f25605529fe92c95961"},{"filename":"fig7_eikonal_verification_yp09_extended.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Contour plots of $\\beta_{\\rm PS}$ for exact numerical $m = \\ell = 10$ PS modes as a function of   $\\{\\calr, \\Theta=\\arctan(a/Q)\\}$ for KNdS families with fixed $y_+ = 0.1$ (top-left panel), $y_+ = 0.5$ (top-right panel) and $y_+ = 0.9$ (bottom panel). In each plot, the critical curve with  $\\beta_{\\rm PS} = \\frac{1}{2}$ is  the red curve (around the top left corner), and the dashed green line identifies the eikonal prediction $\\beta_{\\rm eik}=\\frac{1}{2}$.","label":"fig:eikonal_verification","source":"arxiv","key":"3a5405422119d0832ecc8f939129619d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/3a5405422119d0832ecc8f939129619d"},{"filename":"fig8_eikonal_verification_r095.png","material":"preprint","caption":"{\\bf Left panel:} Density plot of $\\beta_{\\rm PS}$ for PS modes with $m = \\ell = 10$ as a function of $\\Theta$ and $y_+$ for a KNdS slice of constant $\\calr = 0.95$, \\ie a near-extremal KNdS family. {\\bf Right panel:} Critical $\\beta= 1/2$ curves at $\\calr = 0.95$ for different values of $m = \\ell $. Left to right, we have the $\\beta_{\\rm eik}= 1/2$ critical boundary in the eikonal approximation (green dashed line), followed by the numerical $\\beta_{\\rm PS} = 1/2$ curve for $m = \\ell= 10$ (red solid line), $m = \\ell= 2$ (magenta solid line), $m = \\ell = 1$ (brown solid line) and finally the $\\beta_{\\rm NH} = 1/2$ curve for $m = \\ell= 0$ (blue solid line) modes. As we increase the value of $m = \\ell $, we observe that the $\\beta_{\\rm PS} = 1/2$ boundary curve for $m=\\ell$ modes increasingly converges to the $\\beta_{\\rm eik}= 1/2$ curve of the eikonal approximation, which corresponds to $m = \\ell \\to \\infty$.","label":"fig:B0.5contoursform0m2m10andeikonal","source":"arxiv","key":"940788a61179696dd959ea1a192a851e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/940788a61179696dd959ea1a192a851e"},{"filename":"fig8_allbetacontourstogether.png","material":"preprint","caption":"{\\bf Left panel:} Density plot of $\\beta_{\\rm PS}$ for PS modes with $m = \\ell = 10$ as a function of $\\Theta$ and $y_+$ for a KNdS slice of constant $\\calr = 0.95$, \\ie a near-extremal KNdS family. {\\bf Right panel:} Critical $\\beta= 1/2$ curves at $\\calr = 0.95$ for different values of $m = \\ell $. Left to right, we have the $\\beta_{\\rm eik}= 1/2$ critical boundary in the eikonal approximation (green dashed line), followed by the numerical $\\beta_{\\rm PS} = 1/2$ curve for $m = \\ell= 10$ (red solid line), $m = \\ell= 2$ (magenta solid line), $m = \\ell = 1$ (brown solid line) and finally the $\\beta_{\\rm NH} = 1/2$ curve for $m = \\ell= 0$ (blue solid line) modes. As we increase the value of $m = \\ell $, we observe that the $\\beta_{\\rm PS} = 1/2$ boundary curve for $m=\\ell$ modes increasingly converges to the $\\beta_{\\rm eik}= 1/2$ curve of the eikonal approximation, which corresponds to $m = \\ell \\to \\infty$.","label":"fig:B0.5contoursform0m2m10andeikonal","source":"arxiv","key":"5838f0c616ef4f0fdaccc126f966cbde","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5838f0c616ef4f0fdaccc126f966cbde"},{"filename":"fig9_eikonal_verification_3d_extended.png","material":"preprint","caption":"\\textbf{Left panel:} Density plot of $\\beta_{\\rm PS}$ for exact numerical PS modes with $m=\\ell=10$ across the three-dimensional KNdS parameter space $\\{y_+, \\calr, \\Theta=\\arctan(a/Q)\\}$ in the critical region where $\\beta_{\\rm PS}$ crosses the critical value of $1/2$ (red `spherical-like' surface) for $m=\\ell=10$. One has $\\beta_{\\rm PS}>1/2$ in the interior of this red surface. We display contour plots in the vertical plane with $\\Theta = 0$ (RNdS black holes) and in the horizontal plane with $\\calr = 0.98$ (very close to extremal KNdS). \\textbf{Right panel:} Same parameter space region and critical red surface with $\\beta_{\\rm PS}=1/2$ of $m=\\ell=10$ as in the left panel but this time we also display the critical eikonal ($m=\\ell\\to \\infty$) green `spherical-like' surface with $\\beta_{\\rm eik}=1/2$ (inside the red surface).","label":"fig:eikonal_verification3D","source":"arxiv","key":"5030a4c68e88e658f62deadc0bee5297","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5030a4c68e88e658f62deadc0bee5297"},{"filename":"fig9_eikonal_verification_3d_extended_eikonalshell.png","material":"preprint","caption":"\\textbf{Left panel:} Density plot of $\\beta_{\\rm PS}$ for exact numerical PS modes with $m=\\ell=10$ across the three-dimensional KNdS parameter space $\\{y_+, \\calr, \\Theta=\\arctan(a/Q)\\}$ in the critical region where $\\beta_{\\rm PS}$ crosses the critical value of $1/2$ (red `spherical-like' surface) for $m=\\ell=10$. One has $\\beta_{\\rm PS}>1/2$ in the interior of this red surface. We display contour plots in the vertical plane with $\\Theta = 0$ (RNdS black holes) and in the horizontal plane with $\\calr = 0.98$ (very close to extremal KNdS). \\textbf{Right panel:} Same parameter space region and critical red surface with $\\beta_{\\rm PS}=1/2$ of $m=\\ell=10$ as in the left panel but this time we also display the critical eikonal ($m=\\ell\\to \\infty$) green `spherical-like' surface with $\\beta_{\\rm eik}=1/2$ (inside the red surface).","label":"fig:eikonal_verification3D","source":"arxiv","key":"1ef78df6420b54ea445b460449a17756","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/1ef78df6420b54ea445b460449a17756"},{"filename":"fig10_m10psmodestartingatyp05r05.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Comparison at $\\calr = 0.5$, $y_+ = 0.5$, $m = \\ell $ fixed and along $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ of the dominant PS family frequencies (orange circles) against analytic approximations. We have the eikonal approximation $\\omega_{\\hbox{\\tiny eik}}$ from \\eqref{eqn:omega_eik} (green dashed line) and the analytical approximation of PS modes (red dashed line) from~\\cite{Casals:2020uxa}. \\textbf{Left column}: imaginary part of the frequencies for $m = \\ell   = 10$ (top) and $ m = \\ell  =  2$ (bottom). \\textbf{Right column}: real part of the frequencies for $m = \\ell   = 10$ (top) and $ m = \\ell  =  2$ (bottom). These all correspond to $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations on KNdS.","label":"fig:KNdSPSmodeVSWKBandCasals","source":"arxiv","key":"88e9c66efa6f0d7157df90518eea71ba","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/88e9c66efa6f0d7157df90518eea71ba"},{"filename":"fig10_rem10psmodestartingatyp05r05.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Comparison at $\\calr = 0.5$, $y_+ = 0.5$, $m = \\ell $ fixed and along $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ of the dominant PS family frequencies (orange circles) against analytic approximations. We have the eikonal approximation $\\omega_{\\hbox{\\tiny eik}}$ from \\eqref{eqn:omega_eik} (green dashed line) and the analytical approximation of PS modes (red dashed line) from~\\cite{Casals:2020uxa}. \\textbf{Left column}: imaginary part of the frequencies for $m = \\ell   = 10$ (top) and $ m = \\ell  =  2$ (bottom). \\textbf{Right column}: real part of the frequencies for $m = \\ell   = 10$ (top) and $ m = \\ell  =  2$ (bottom). These all correspond to $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations on KNdS.","label":"fig:KNdSPSmodeVSWKBandCasals","source":"arxiv","key":"2205ef28daa224d706d623c2534dd18a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/2205ef28daa224d706d623c2534dd18a"},{"filename":"fig10_m2psmodestartingatyp05r05.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Comparison at $\\calr = 0.5$, $y_+ = 0.5$, $m = \\ell $ fixed and along $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ of the dominant PS family frequencies (orange circles) against analytic approximations. We have the eikonal approximation $\\omega_{\\hbox{\\tiny eik}}$ from \\eqref{eqn:omega_eik} (green dashed line) and the analytical approximation of PS modes (red dashed line) from~\\cite{Casals:2020uxa}. \\textbf{Left column}: imaginary part of the frequencies for $m = \\ell   = 10$ (top) and $ m = \\ell  =  2$ (bottom). \\textbf{Right column}: real part of the frequencies for $m = \\ell   = 10$ (top) and $ m = \\ell  =  2$ (bottom). These all correspond to $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations on KNdS.","label":"fig:KNdSPSmodeVSWKBandCasals","source":"arxiv","key":"0cc7ee966690526e29f8acd13769c734","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/0cc7ee966690526e29f8acd13769c734"},{"filename":"fig10_rem2psmodestartingatyp05r05.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Comparison at $\\calr = 0.5$, $y_+ = 0.5$, $m = \\ell $ fixed and along $\\Theta = \\arctan(a/Q)$ of the dominant PS family frequencies (orange circles) against analytic approximations. We have the eikonal approximation $\\omega_{\\hbox{\\tiny eik}}$ from \\eqref{eqn:omega_eik} (green dashed line) and the analytical approximation of PS modes (red dashed line) from~\\cite{Casals:2020uxa}. \\textbf{Left column}: imaginary part of the frequencies for $m = \\ell   = 10$ (top) and $ m = \\ell  =  2$ (bottom). \\textbf{Right column}: real part of the frequencies for $m = \\ell   = 10$ (top) and $ m = \\ell  =  2$ (bottom). These all correspond to $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations on KNdS.","label":"fig:KNdSPSmodeVSWKBandCasals","source":"arxiv","key":"9bd49735a480239dc1a12ef5576a8d91","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/9bd49735a480239dc1a12ef5576a8d91"},{"filename":"fig11_rndsyp05r09tor099nhmaecardoso.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Imaginary part of the frequency of near-horizon (NH) modes for scalar field QNMs with $q =\\mu = 0$ and $m = \\ell = 2, n=0$ for near-extremal  RNdS as a function of $\\calr \\in [0.9, 0.99]$  for three distinct RNdS families with fixed $y_+$, namely $y_+ = 0.25$ (top left), $y_+ = 0.5$ (top right) and $y_+ = 0.75$ (bottom).  The numerical exact NH modes are represented by the blue diamonds, the $n = 0$ MAE approximation $\\omega_{\\hbox{\\tiny MAE}}$ is described by the purple dot-dashed line and the near-horizon approximation $\\omega_{[2]}$ of~\\cite{Cardoso:2017soq} by the yellow dot-dashed line.","label":"fig:cardosoNHapproximationVSMAE","source":"arxiv","key":"9cad0ffc0c4f4f7e5aa1608accdbb95a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/9cad0ffc0c4f4f7e5aa1608accdbb95a"},{"filename":"fig12_imkndsyp09rfrom095to09999.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Value of $\\beta$ for the dominant NH mode (blue diamonds) for $\\mathcal{R} \\in [0.95, 0.9999]$ for $q =\\mu = 0$ linear scalar perturbations with $m = \\ell = 0$ on KNdS with $\\Theta = \\pi/4 \\sim 0.79,\\, y_+ = 0.9$. The $n = 0$ ($n=1$) MAE approximation $\\omega_{\\MAE}$ to the NH modes is given by a dot-dashed purple curve. The red (yellow) horizontal line indicates $\\beta = \\frac{1}{2}$ ($\\beta = 1$).","label":"fig:NH_ml0_R095_to_R09999_yp09_beta","source":"arxiv","key":"fea18c50c7254ca8e5449556ad62902e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/fea18c50c7254ca8e5449556ad62902e"},{"filename":"fig13_convergencetest_r095_res200to600_precfixed.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Numerical converge tests of $\\beta$ for KNdS at $y_+ = 0.9$, $R = 0.95$, with $m = \\ell = 10$ and distinct $\\Theta$ values. These modes were converged for a fixed numerical precision of 100 digits and a range of resolutions, starting at a minimum of 200 grid points and then linearly scaling up to a 600 grid points. The maximum values were used to compute the reference value $\\beta_{\\text{ref}}$.","label":"fig:convergenceplot","source":"arxiv","key":"c42b3a24e980263a6d22b8864efa6b8e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/c42b3a24e980263a6d22b8864efa6b8e"}],"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology","source":"arxiv"},{"term":"Theory-HEP","source":"arxiv"}],"first_author":{"emails":["amd1g13@soton.ac.uk"],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"GRID","value":"grid.5491.9"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/01ryk1543"}],"full_name":"Davey, Alex","ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2271-4501"},{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"A.Davey.1"}],"last_name":"Davey","first_name":"Alex","recid":1892901},"control_number":2774616,"dois":[{"source":"Springer","value":"10.1007/JHEP07(2024)113"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Davey:2024xvd"],"abstracts":[{"source":"Springer","value":"Christodoulou’s formulation of Strong Cosmic Censorship (SCC) holds true for Kerr-de Sitter black holes. On the other hand, Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter black holes violate SCC. We do a detailed scan of the parameter space of Kerr-Newman-de Sitter black holes between these two limiting families, to identify the boundary that marks the transition between solutions that respect and violate SCC. We focus our attention on linear scalar field perturbations. SCC is violated inside a (roughly) ‘spherical’ shell of the parameter space of Kerr-Newman-de Sitter, centred at the corner that describes arbitrarily small extremal Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter solutions. Outside of this region, including the Kerr-de Sitter limit, we identify perturbation modes that decay slow enough to enforce SCC. Additionally, we do a necessary study of the quasinormal mode spectra of Kerr-Newman-de Sitter in some detail. As established in the literature, in the Kerr-de Sitter and Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter limits, we find three families of modes: de Sitter, photon sphere and near-horizon modes. These interact non-trivially away from the Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter limit and display eigenvalue repulsions like in Kerr-Newman black holes.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Springer"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"Christodoulou's formulation of Strong Cosmic Censorship (SCC) holds true for Kerr-de Sitter black holes. On the other hand, Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter black holes violate SCC. We do a detailed scan of the parameter space of Kerr-Newman-de Sitter black holes between these two limiting families, to identify the boundary that marks the transition between solutions that respect and violate SCC. We focus our attention on linear scalar field perturbations. SCC is violated inside a (roughly) `spherical' shell of the parameter space of Kerr-Newman-de Sitter, centred at the corner that describes arbitrarily small extremal Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter solutions. Outside of this region, including the Kerr-de Sitter limit, we identify perturbation modes that decay slow enough to enforce SCC. Additionally, we do a necessary study of the quasinormal mode spectra of Kerr-Newman-de Sitter in some detail. As established in the literature, in the Kerr-de Sitter and Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter limits, we find three families of modes: de Sitter, photon sphere and near-horizon modes. These interact non-trivially away from the Reissner-Nordström-de Sitter limit and display eigenvalue repulsions like in Kerr-Newman black holes.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"source":"Springer","title":"Strong Cosmic Censorship in Kerr-Newman-de Sitter"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Strong Cosmic Censorship in Kerr-Newman-de Sitter"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2024-07-11"}],"curated":true},"id":"2774616","created":"2024-04-08T03:41:01.008893+00:00"},{"updated":"2026-08-18T13:24:58.250427+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3149804?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3149804?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3149804?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3149804?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3149804?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3149804?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A3149804"},"metadata":{"authors":[{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"al-badawi, ahmad","full_name":"Al-Badawi, Ahmad","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1058532"},"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"A.Al.Badawi.1"}],"last_name":"Al-Badawi","first_name":"Ahmad","uuid":"5247f4b7-025c-4743-a361-2edbb795aad2","recid":1058532},{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"ahmed, faizuddin","full_name":"Ahmed, Faizuddin","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1648375"},"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"Faizuddin.Ahmed.1"}],"last_name":"Ahmed","first_name":"Faizuddin","uuid":"038fa2d3-a196-49c8-9389-bf8829dca97d","recid":1648375},{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"sakallı, i̇zzet","full_name":"Sakallı, İzzet","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1047854"},"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"I.Sakalli.1"}],"last_name":"Sakallı","first_name":"İzzet","uuid":"1c2cc2d5-40b0-46f6-b91a-24ef1970e3f2","recid":1047854}],"citation_count_without_self_citations":1,"citation_count":1,"citeable":true,"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","references":[{"reference":{"label":"1","publication_info":{"year":1979},"title":{"title":"GENERAL RELATIVITY. AN EINSTEIN CENTENARY SURVEY"},"misc":["Ultraviolet divergences in quantum theories of gravitation. pages 790-831"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Weinberg, S."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[1] S. Weinberg. Ultraviolet divergences in quantum theories of gravitation. General Relativity: An Einstein Centenary Survey, pages 790–831, 1979."}]},{"reference":{"dois":["10.1103/PhysRevD.57.971"],"label":"2","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"57","page_end":"985","year":1998,"page_start":"971","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D"},"misc":["Nonperturbative evolution equation for quantum gravity","URL https: //doi.org/"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Reuter, M."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[2] M. Reuter. Nonperturbative evolution equation for quantum gravity. Phys. Rev. D, 57:971–985, 1998. URL https: //doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.57.971."}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/418310"}},{"reference":{"dois":["10.12942/lrr-2006-5"],"label":"3","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"9","artid":"5","year":2006,"page_start":"5","journal_title":"Living Rev.Rel."},"misc":["The Asymptotic Safety Scenario in Quantum Gravity","URL"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Niedermaier, M."},{"full_name":"Reuter, M."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","source":"arXiv","value":"[3] M. Niedermaier and M. Reuter. The Asymptotic Safety Scenario in Quantum Gravity. Living Rev. Rel., 9:5, 2006. URL https://doi.org/10.12942/lrr-2006-5."}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/719313"}},{"reference":{"dois":["10.1103/PhysRevD.62.043008"],"label":"4","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"62","artid":"043008","year":2000,"journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D"},"misc":["Renormalization group improved black hole space-times","URL"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Bonanno, A."},{"full_name":"Reuter